"use strict";(self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara=self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara||[]).push([["42752"],{27625(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"FIN-073","slug":"credit","term":"Credit","also_known_as":[],"difficulty":"Beginner","definition":"Credit is the ability to borrow money or buy goods and services now, with an agreement to pay later. It also describes the trust a lender places in a borrower to repay.","explanation":["When a shop lets you take goods and pay at the end of the month, or a bank lets you borrow up to a limit on a credit card, you are using credit. The lender is trusting that you will pay back what you owe, usually with interest.","Credit comes in two broad forms. Instalment credit, such as a car loan or mortgage, involves borrowing a fixed amount and repaying it in regular instalments. Revolving credit, such as a credit card or line of credit, lets you borrow repeatedly up to a limit, repay and borrow again.","Your access to credit depends on your creditworthiness: how likely lenders believe you are to repay. They judge this from your income, existing debts and credit history, often summarised in a credit score. Good credit usually means easier approval and lower interest rates.","Used carefully, credit can help spread the cost of large purchases and build a positive credit record. Used carelessly, it can lead to expensive debt.","The word \\"credit\\" also has a separate meaning in bookkeeping, where it is the right-hand entry in an account and the opposite of a debit. That meaning is explained in the Debit entry."],"note":"","total":1500,"prev":{"slug":"covered-call","term":"Covered Call"},"next":{"slug":"credit-analysis","term":"Credit Analysis"}}')}}]);